How OVERWATCH distinguishes child costs within a stored procedure
The key mechanism is QUERY_ATTRIBUTION_HISTORY.ROOT_QUERY_ID. When you CALL MY_PROC() and that proc internally runs 5 SQL statements, Snowflake stamps all 5 child statements with ROOT_QUERY_ID = the CALL's QUERY_ID. OVERWATCH exploits this at three levels:

1. Aggregate leaderboard (procedure_costs_usd)
Groups all CALL statements, rolls up every child's attributed credits to the parent CALL via ROOT_QUERY_ID, and produces a $/call leaderboard ranked by total cost. This answers: "Which stored procedures are most expensive?"

2. Per-CALL lookup (call_cost_lookup)
Given a specific QUERY_ID or even just a SESSION_ID (for the "three procs in one session, no graph id" case), it finds every CALL in that session and sums each one's children independently. This answers: "I called proc A, then proc B, then proc C in one session — what did each cost?"

3. Child breakdown drill-down (call_children_costs)
For ONE specific CALL, it lists every child statement with its own attributed credits, query type, and a text preview. This answers: "Inside this one CALL, which SQL statements burned the money?"

The relevant code is at app/data/insights_sql.py:891-1002.

What this means for your scenario
If you run a session that calls multiple stored procedures:

sql
CALL PROC_A();   -- QUERY_ID = 'aaa'
CALL PROC_B();   -- QUERY_ID = 'bbb'  
CALL PROC_C();   -- QUERY_ID = 'ccc'
Each child statement inside PROC_A gets ROOT_QUERY_ID = 'aaa', PROC_B's children get ROOT_QUERY_ID = 'bbb', etc. OVERWATCH's call_cost_lookup with the SESSION_ID will show three distinct rows with independent credit totals. Then call_children_costs('aaa') drills into PROC_A's internals.

Limitations / caveats
~6-8 hour attribution lag — QUERY_ATTRIBUTION_HISTORY is not real-time.
Idle time excluded — attribution credits answer "what did running THIS cost," not "what share of the warehouse bill does this own." The idle-inclusive allocated view is a separate surface.
Nested CALLs — If PROC_A calls PROC_B internally, all children still roll up to the outermost CALL's ROOT_QUERY_ID. You can distinguish them by QUERY_TYPE in the children breakdown, but there's no native multi-level tree in the current OVERWATCH UI.
QAS credits were missing (finding C2 from the audit) — the procedure_costs_usd builder originally summed only CREDITS_ATTRIBUTED_COMPUTE, dropping QAS. The call_children_costs builder was already fixed to include QAS, but the leaderboard and trend are not yet patched.
So yes, OVERWATCH already differentiates per-proc costs within a session and per-statement costs within a proc — it's one of the measured unit-cost surfaces in the "Cost & Contract > Unit Costs" tab.



